sections.s2_technical:
Lambda shines for event-driven and intermittent work. When an S3 upload, an SQS message, an API Gateway request, or an EventBridge event occurs, Lambda runs your function, scales automatically with the number of events, and you pay only for the milliseconds it runs. When nothing happens, it costs nothing, which is perfect for a job that runs a few times a day or has unpredictable spikes. A container on ECS or EKS, by contrast, is a process you keep running, so you pay for it continuously even when idle, which is cheaper only when utilization is high and steady.

The first constraint is the cold start. When Lambda needs a fresh execution environment — the first invocation, or when scaling out — it must download your code, start the runtime, and initialize, adding latency from tens of milliseconds to a few seconds depending on language and package size. Subsequent invocations reuse a warm environment and are fast. For a Java function this matters more because the JVM takes time to start, which you mitigate with provisioned concurrency, SnapStart, smaller packages, and lightweight frameworks.

```java
// Initialize expensive clients ONCE outside the handler so warm invocations reuse them.
public class FeeCalculatorHandler {
    // Created on cold start, reused on warm invocations (not re-created per call).
    private static final ExpensiveClient client = ExpensiveClient.create();

    public String handleRequest(Map<String,Object> event, Object context) {
        return client.process(event); // fast on warm starts
    }
}
```

The second constraint is the maximum execution time of fifteen minutes. Lambda is not for a process that runs for hours; a long batch job must be a container or be broken into steps, for example with Step Functions.

The third constraint is memory and CPU coupling. You configure memory, and CPU scales in proportion automatically — you cannot set CPU independently. So a CPU-bound function is sometimes given more memory than it needs just to get more CPU, which affects cost and design.

The fourth constraint is statelessness. Each invocation may run in a different execution environment, and you cannot rely on local disk or in-memory state surviving between invocations. Any state must go to an external store like DynamoDB, S3, or ElastiCache. The small writable `/tmp` space is ephemeral and only sometimes reused on warm environments.

A senior nuance: there are also limits on payload size, concurrent executions per account, and deployment package size, and very chatty synchronous call chains of Lambdas can be both slow and costly. So I reach for Lambda when the work is genuinely event-driven and short, and I keep a container when the workload is long-running, steady, needs predictable low latency, or exceeds these limits.

